  "query_preview": "..\n\nSYNOPTIC SETUP:\n\nA low pressure system over northern California will track east\ninto northern Wyoming into the weekend. This system is cutoff by a\nnorthern stream shallow ridge in Canada which is leading to dry\nair cutting off the northern extent of the precipitation for\nCanadian border regions. Latest NBM forecast QPF shows a much \nsharper gradient over NE Montana as a result with near little \nprecipitation near the Canadian border. The heaviest totals in \nexcess of 1.00\" are likely south of Highway 200 from Winnett to \nGlendive as well as south of I-94 through Wibaux. Across these \nsouthern locations, heavier bands of showers and embedded \nthunderstorms that remain persistent enough may produce minor \nflooding of small streams, open fields, and poor drainage \nlocations such as low lying areas. \n\nAt this time, confidence in exact precipitation totals through\nSunday for NE Montana remains below average given the sharp \ncutoff to the north, southward trend, and dynamic nature of this \nsystem. Thus, will hold off on any flood watches for now. \nConfidence is greater to the south of the CWA closer to the storm \ntrack. The WPC maintains Marginal/Slight Risks for Excessive \nRainfall Outlook products over much of Montana for Day 1 and 2.\nThose traveling across the state should monitor the latest\nforecast as well as watch and warning products issued by\nrespective weather service offices.\n\nThe storm system looks to exit Sunday into Monday with ensembles\nshowing the northern stream ridge having a bit more influence over\nconditions. As a result look for a warm up in temperatures and\ndrier weather into early next week.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nModels continue to show poor run to run continuity in\nprecipitation amounts for NE Montana this weekend, with the\ngradient tightening across the area and a southward shift. Thus,\nconfidence remains below average for rainfall totals, but\nincreases for southern tier zones closer to the storm track. Areas\nsouth of Highway 200 from Wibaux to Glendive and south of I-94\nthrough Wibaux may experience heavier rainfall totals and the\npotential for minor flooding.",
